{"title":"Breaking one factor into equal parts","key_idea":"You can split one factor into smaller factors and keep both sides of the equation equal.","method":["Look at the factor you need to split.","Find two smaller numbers that multiply to give that factor.","Write the equation with the split factors on one side and fill in the missing number."],"worked_example":{"problem":"5 x 10 = 5 x 2 x __","steps":["The factor 10 needs to be split into two smaller numbers.","Find two numbers that multiply to make 10: 2 and 5.","Since we already wrote 2, the missing number is 5."],"answer":"5 x 10 = 5 x 2 x 5"},"key_terms":[{"term":"factor","meaning":"a number you multiply by another number"},{"term":"split or break down","meaning":"divide a number into smaller numbers that multiply back to the original"}],"common_mistakes":["Choosing two numbers that don't multiply to give the original factor.","Forgetting that the two smaller numbers must multiply together to equal the factor being split."],"remember":"If you split one factor into two smaller factors, the answer stays the same on both sides.","quick_check":[{"q":"7 x 15 = 7 x 3 x __","a":"5, because 3 x 5 = 15"},{"q":"4 x 20 = 4 x 5 x __","a":"4, because 5 x 4 = 20"}],"tell_your_child":"When you see a number you need to split, think of two smaller numbers that multiply together to make it. Then your equation will stay balanced on both sides.","picture":{"concept_key":"c5-math-factor-splitting","scene_prompt":"A child arranging 10 toys into two smaller groups, one with 2 toys and another with 5 toys arranged side by side."}}
